<!-- NEUROFIT / 06 EVALUATION & DESIGN TRADE-OFFS -->
<section class="project-section storytelling-section nf-evaluation-section" id="evaluation">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="row align-items-start g-5">
      <div class="col-lg-5">
        <p class="description">06 / Evaluation &amp; Design Trade-offs</p>
        <h2>Refining the experience through stakeholder feedback</h2>
        <p>We reviewed design decisions with Neurofit's CEO to align the proposed experience with audience needs, business priorities, and implementation constraints. Feedback informed changes to credibility signals, content framing, and page priorities.</p>
        <p>These reviews supported design refinement, but they were not a substitute for usability testing with representative end users.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="col-lg-7"><div class="nf-decision-list">
        <div><span>01 / CREDIBILITY</span><h3>From internal metrics to external proof</h3><p>On the homepage, the proposed “Our Progress” metrics were replaced with partner and collaborator logos to provide more recognizable credibility signals.</p></div>
        <div><span>02 / AUDIENCE RELEVANCE</span><h3>From features to benefits</h3><p>For insurers and TPAs, messaging shifted toward outcomes and reporting value. A static dashboard image carousel replaced the proposed demo video, reflecting how these professionals evaluate the platform.</p></div>
        <div><span>03 / SCOPE JUDGMENT</span><h3>Why we removed the pricing page</h3><p>We explored a pricing page at mid fidelity, but removed it from the final proposed structure because pricing tiers were still evolving and billing occurred inside the product. Publishing incomplete information risked creating misleading expectations.</p></div>
      </div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="nf-testing-limitations mt-5">
      <span class="nf-testing-eyebrow">RESEARCH INTEGRITY / LIMITATION</span>
      <h3>Usability testing was planned, but not completed</h3>
      <p>We planned moderated, think-aloud testing with clinicians and insurers/TPAs and began recruitment ahead of the testing window. Because participants required specialized professional expertise, recruitment relied heavily on client introductions. A potential participant was identified late in the project, but a session could not be arranged before the deadline.</p>
      <p>We chose not to substitute classmates or friends for domain specialists. Consequently, the final designs were reviewed with the stakeholder but <strong>were not validated through formal usability testing</strong>; usability and conversion improvements remain hypotheses for future evaluation.</p>
      <p class="mb-0"><strong>Next step:</strong> Conduct task-based sessions with both audiences to assess product comprehension, navigation, evidence discoverability, and free-trial pathways.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

